# Project-local npm configuration.
#
# The npm cache is kept inside the workspace so installs work in sandboxed
# environments where the global %LOCALAPPDATA%\npm-cache is not writable.
cache=.cache/npm

# Electron and electron-builder binary mirrors are configured through the
# environment, not here:
#
#   ELECTRON_MIRROR=https://npmmirror.com/mirrors/electron/
#   ELECTRON_BUILDER_BINARIES_MIRROR=https://npmmirror.com/mirrors/electron-builder-binaries/
#
# Two reasons they left this file. npm 11 warns that unknown .npmrc keys stop
# being passed to lifecycle scripts in the next major, and these keys only work
# at all because npm exports them as npm_config_* — `@electron/get` reads that
# name first, then ELECTRON_MIRROR, so the environment variable is the form that
# cannot be taken away by an npm upgrade. And a committed mirror silently sends
# every contributor's binary downloads through one third party; this repo should
# default to the official GitHub releases and let a machine opt in.
#
# The mirror is load-bearing where GitHub release assets are unreachable. Note
# that git operations succeeding is not the same test: fetching, ls-remote and
# the API can all work while a release-asset download is reset mid-transfer.
# Check with:
#
#   curl -L -r 0-0 -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' \
#     https://github.com/electron/electron/releases/download/v38.2.2/electron-v38.2.2-win32-x64.zip
#
# and re-run `node node_modules/electron/install.js` after changing it.
